FIGURE PARAMETERS
- Panels (a) and (b): |q|=1 and arg(q)=arg(F)=0; the plotted square-root
  choice has arg(s)=(arg(q)+pi)/2=pi/2.  Choosing -s merely interchanges t and
  -t and the sheet labels.
- Panel (c): |q|=1 and arg(F)=arg(q) in {0, pi/4, pi/2}, in fixed Bargmann
  z coordinates.
- Plot ranges: [-1.13,1.13] on each complex-plane axis ([-1.10,1.10] for the
  three compact comparison insets), all with equal aspect ratio.
- Output canvas: 7.15 x 5.35 inches.  PNG resolution: 450 dpi.
- Deterministic rendering; no random elements.
- Blue (#35618d) and orange (#b66a3c) shading identify representative
  dominance sectors.  Curve types are additionally distinguished by dashed
  versus solid strokes, so colour is not the sole encoding.

FORMULAS AND CONVENTION IMPLEMENTED
- z=t^2, s^2=-q.
- S_+(t)=+2st, S_-(t)=-2st, Delta S=S_+-S_-=4st.
- Equal-modulus curves: Re(Delta S)=0, equivalently Re(st)=0.  These are
  called Stokes curves in this manuscript and are drawn dashed.
- Constant-phase curves: Im(Delta S)=0, equivalently Im(st)=0.  These are
  called anti-Stokes curves in this manuscript and are drawn solid.
- The equations, rather than the potentially non-universal names, explicitly
  define the convention in code labels, the note, manuscript text, and caption.
- With q=|q| exp(i phi) and the displayed choice
  arg(s)=(phi+pi)/2, the t-plane equal-modulus line has direction
  pi/2-arg(s), and the constant-phase line has direction -arg(s).
- Under z=t^2, angles double.  Opposite t-rays are therefore identified and
  map to the single physical z-rays theta=-phi (equal modulus) and
  theta=pi-phi (constant phase), modulo 2pi.
- Re(st)>0 makes exp(S_+) dominant; Re(st)<0 makes exp(S_-) dominant.
- The marked t and -t demonstrate the two sheets above the same z=t^2.
- z=0 is explicitly marked as the branch point in the physical plane.

PHYSICAL INTERPRETATION
The figure and text explicitly state
U_chi H(F) U_chi^dagger = H(F exp(i chi)), U_chi=exp(i chi N).
Thus all values of arg(F) are physically unitarily equivalent: the spectrum
depends on |F|, not arg(F).  Changing arg(F)=arg(q) nevertheless rotates the
sector geometry when the Bargmann coordinate frame is held fixed.  No claim
that this rotation changes the spectrum is made.

ROLE OF |q|
Ray angles use only arg(q).  |q| was fixed at 1 in every panel and was never
allowed to change ray positions.  Since |s|=sqrt(|q|), changing |q| only
rescales Re(Delta S)=4 Re(st), hence the strength of exponential dominance.
The figure represents dominance by restrained sector shading and does not
misrepresent |q| as an angular effect.

