VISUAL INSPECTION AND CORRECTIONS
The standalone 3269 x 2459 PNG was opened with the image viewer at original
and reduced detail.  The canonical manuscript PDF pages 15, 16, and 17 were
then rasterized at 140 dpi and all three were opened together.  Page 16 holds
the figure and caption; pages 15 and 17 are its immediate neighbors.

The first standalone inspection found lost backslashes in several math labels,
overlap among the branch-point and ray labels in panel (a), and a collision
between the legend and the unitary-equivalence statement in panel (c).
Corrections restored operator/argument math commands, moved the branch and ray
labels, adjusted the sheet-II label, changed the legend to two columns, and
separated the legend from the equivalence statement.  A second inspection led
to one final upward shift of the branch-point label.  The outputs were then
regenerated and the manuscript rebuilt.  Final inspection found the panels,
line styles, sector shading, sheet mapping, caption, and surrounding pages
clear, unclipped, and free of misleading branch information.

WARNINGS, APPROXIMATIONS, LIMITATIONS, UNRESOLVED ISSUES
- The initial Python environment combined NumPy 2.2.6 with a Matplotlib binary
  built against NumPy 1.x.  The compatible deterministic plotting environment
  was restored by installing NumPy 1.26.4 and Matplotlib 3.11.1.  Matplotlib's
  unwritable default cache warning was avoided with MPLCONFIGDIR under /tmp.
- Dominance shading is qualitative; it identifies the sign of Re(st), not a
  numerically calibrated magnitude.  This is intentional because the stage is
  limited to angular geometry.
- The plotted choice of square root s is conventional; changing s to -s swaps
  sheet/exponential labels without changing the physical z-plane rays.
- No unresolved figure, manuscript-build, test, clipping, or reference issue
  remains.

